      "textEn": "86\nShe was not at all satisfied with her father's silence. Sati was Daksha's youngest daughter, and she knew that\nshe was his most beloved daughter. But now, due to Shiva's association, the father had forgotten all of his daughter's\nlove, which caused her immense pain. The physical body-mind-ego is so corrupted\nthat even with a little provocation, all the bonds of our love and affection break. Physical relationships are so\ntransient that even if someone has loved someone so much, in a little bit of resentment,\nall intimacy is shattered.\nThe one who is without anger, who is without pride,\nWho is without the slightest fault in the sacrifices performed by the father and the gods,\nThe one who is without obstacles, the lord of the sacrifices,\nShe is angry and drives away the wicked. 9>. Meaning of the word:\nArudra-bhagam--not receiving the share of Shiva's Yagya; tam--that; aveekshya-without seeing; cha--and; adhvaram--the place of Yagya; pitra--father; by; cha--and; deve-of Shiva; krit-hailanam--by disrespecting; vibhau--of the master; anahata-disrespected; yagya-\nsadasi-in the assembly of Yagya; adhishwari-sati; chupok-very angry; lokan-fourteen realms; iv-as if; dhakhyati-burning; rusha-in anger;\njalati-burning; rusha-in anger.. Then they realized that their father had not invited Shiva; Soe, when Daksha\nsaw Shiva's venerable wife, he did not respect her either. Therefore, she became extremely angry.\nShe even started looking at her father in such a way as if she would turn him to ashes with her eyes.\nMeaning: When offerings are made into the fire while chanting the Vedic mantra Swaha,\nall the gods, sages, and ancestors, including Brahma, Shiva, and Vishnu, are\nrespectfully honored. It is traditionally believed that Shiva is one of the respected ones among the respected ones. But Sati\nHaving appeared at the sacrificial site, saw that the Brahmin people were not offering oblations to Shiva by chanting the mantra 'Namah Shivaya Swaha'. She was not sad for herself, because she was ready to come to her father's house without invitation,\nbut she wanted to see if her husband was being respected or not. Own"
