ही ग्रहण करते हैं, उनके\nदोषों पर विचार नहीं करते, किन्तु सामान्य मनुष्य गुण तथा दोषों का निर्णय कर सकता हे ।",
      "textEn": "89\nFor the sake of equality. Similarly, Shiva is a qualitative incarnation of God, therefore, he has the same qualities as God.\nSimilar qualities are found in him. Therefore, they are impartial, neither are they a friend, nor are they an enemy, but one who\nby nature is malicious can become an enemy of Shiva. Therefore, Sati convicted her father.\nShe said, \"Apart from you, who else would be jealous of Shiva or become his enemy?\"Other monks and learned Brahmins were present there, but they were not jealous of Shiva, although they were all\ndependent on Daksha. Therefore, besides Daksha, why should anyone else start quarreling with Shiva! This was Sati's\nblame.\nThose who are not faulty, they are virtuous in their qualities.\nSome are ignorant of the qualities, O twice-born.\nThey are the best among them, they are not ignorant. 12>. Meaning of the word\nDosha-- fault; Paraesham-- others; Hi-- because; Guneshu-- in qualities; Sadhavah-- virtuous; Grihanti-- obtain; Ketach- some;\nNa-- not; Bhavaadrishah-- like you; Dwija-- hey Dwija; Gunan- qualities; Cha-- and; Phalgun- small; Bahuli- having many; Karishnavah- those who are like that; Tesham- from them; Avidat- they search; Bhavan- you; Agham- fault, error..\nOh, wise Daksha, only a person like you can find faults in the qualities of others. But Shiva does not find fault in the qualities of others, on the contrary, if someone has even a little quality, then they\nincrease it even more. Unfortunately, you have blamed such a great man. Meaning: Here, Daksha is addressed by his daughter Sati as 'Dwija'.\nOf the twice-born humans,\nindicative of the best castes - Brahmin, Kshatriya, and Vaishya ©. In other words, a twice-born person is not\na common person, but a person who has studied Vedic literature from a guru and who has good and\ndistinguished between good and bad. Therefore, it is hoped that he understands logic and philosophy. Dakshakanya\nSati presented strong arguments before Daksha. There are some great men who only accept the qualities of others. Just as a bee is concerned with the nectar of flowers, not with their thorns and colors,\nin the same way, a highly capable person, who is rare, only accepts the qualities of others, does not consider their\nflaws, but a common man can judge the qualities and flaws."
