      "textEn": "93\nWho; the one who completes; Cha--and; Ashish:--desires; Arthin:--seeking; Tasmai-that; Bhavan--you; Drushyati Droha\nThey do; Vishva-Bandhave--to the friend of all the souls of the three worlds.. They are the ones who fulfill all the desires of ordinary\nmen, and also bless those great men who seek Brahmananda (divine bliss),\nwho remain absorbed in the meditation of their lotus feet. Note: There are two categories of humans in general. One is those who are completely materialistic,\nare lustful for material possessions, and whose desires are fulfilled by worshiping Shiva. Because of being Ashutosh,\nShivji is the one who fulfills the material desires of the common people, therefore it is seen\nthat the common people are especially eager to worship him.\nThe second are those people who are tired of physical life,\nthey worship Shivji to attain salvation, which means liberation from bodily consciousness. He who understands that he is not the body, but the soul, is liberated from ignorance. Lord Shiva\nalso provides this facility. Generally, people adopt religion to earn wealth by economic development,\nbecause with wealth, they can satisfy their senses. But when they get bored of life,\nthey desire spiritual Brahmananda, that is, they want to be one with God. Dharma, Artha, Kama,\nand Moksha, these four are the means of material life, and Lord Shiva is equally a friend to the common people as well as to the advanced people in spiritual\nknowledge. Therefore, it was not appropriate for Daksha to be hostile towards them. Here,\neven the Vaishnavas, who are superior to the common and advanced people of this world, worship Shiva as the best\nas Vaishnava.\nIn this way, they are friends of the wise--whether they are common people, advanced people, or\ndevotees of God--therefore, no one should disrespect Shiva or be hostile to him.\nShould not. Who is called Shiva, who is Shiva, do not know others.\nBrahma and others, having removed their matted hair, came to the cremation ground.\nThat garland, ashes, skulls, serpents, and the sun,\nThey place on their heads, that which is created. 16. Shabdharam"
