      "textEn": "96\nSacrificed the body, as one finds in the following verses. Having cut off the ears, the desire for liberation from the cycle of birth and death,\nThe unrighteousness of the gods, the creation of the gods,\nThe destruction of the senses, the destruction of the senses,\nThe Lord, even with his tongue, does not destroy the righteousness. 97. Meaning of the word\n\nKarnaau - both ears; Pidhay - to close; Nirayaat - should go; Yat - if; Akalpah - incapable; Ishe - master; Dharma-\nAvitari - controller of Dharma; Asrunibhih - unrestrained; Nribhih - person; Asyamane - stained; Chhindyat - to cut off;\nChahiye; Pasahya - forcefully; SIH - one who criticizes; Astitim - one who is criticized; Prabhuh - capable; Cheet - if; Jihvam - tongue;\nAsun - (one's) life; Api - certainly; Tat - yours; Visrijet - should be abandoned; Sah - he; Dharmah - is the rule..\n\nSati further said: If someone hears someone criticizing a tyrannical person as the master and controller of religion,\nand if he is not able to punish him, then he should cover his ears and\nleave from there.\nBut if he is capable of killing, then he should forcefully cut off the tongue of the despicable\nand kill the criminal. Then he too should sacrifice his life. Meaning: The argument that Sati gave is that the man who criticizes a great man,\nHe is vile. But with the same logic, Daksha could have clarified by saying that because of being a Prajapati,\nhis position was so prestigious that instead of criticizing Sati, she could accept his qualities. But the answer to this argument is that Sati was not criticizing, she was just clarifying. If\nit were possible, that Daksha would have cut off Kaush's tongue, because he had often criticized Shiva. In other words,\nsince Shiva is the protector of Dharma, therefore whoever criticizes him should be immediately killed\nand after killing such a person, one should give up one's own life. That is the law.\nBut\nDaksha was Sati's father, therefore, instead of